import java.util.*;

class HexToDecimal
{
    public static void main(String args[])
    {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
        System.out.print("Enter a hexadecimal digit: ");
        char ch = sc.next().charAt(0);

        Character obj = ch;          // autoboxing: char -> Character
        char c = obj;                // unboxing: Character -> char
        c = Character.toUpperCase(c);

        int d;
        if (c >= '0' && c <= '9')
            d = c - '0';
        else if (c >= 'A' && c <= 'F')
            d = c - 'A' + 10;
        else
        {
            System.out.println("Invalid hexadecimal digit");
            return;
        }

        Integer value = d;           // autoboxing: int -> Integer
        int result = value;          // unboxing: Integer -> int
        System.out.println("Decimal value = " + result);
    }
}

// Variable description
// ch     : char - character entered
// obj    : Character - boxed form of ch
// c      : char - unboxed, uppercase character
// d      : int - decimal value
// value  : Integer - boxed decimal value
// result : int - unboxed decimal value printed

A local variable is declared inside a method (or block). It can be used only inside that method/block and is destroyed when the method ends.
Example:
void sum()
{
    int a = 5, b = 10, c;
    c = a + b;
    System.out.print(c);
}
Here a, b and c are local to sum() and cannot be used in any other method.

Instance variables are declared in the class body, outside every method and constructor, without the keyword static. Each object gets its own copy.
Example:
class Abc
{
    int num1;
    double num2;
}
num1 and num2 are instance variables.

questions.question:
Find the errors and correct them:
class Data {
    int result(int x, int y) {
        int z = x * y;
        return z;
    }
    double compute(int a, int b) {
        double c;
        c = a * b * z / a;
        return c;
    }
    void display() {
        System.out.println(z);
        d = compute(5, 10);
        System.out.println(c);
    }
}

questions.solution:
Errors (all are scope errors):
1. c = a * b * z / a;  z is local to result(), so it is not known in compute(). Correction: c = a * b * result(a, b) / a; (or c = a * b / a;)
2. System.out.println(z);  z is not known in display(). Correction: System.out.println(result(5, 10));
3. d = compute(5, 10);  d is never declared. Correction: double d = compute(5, 10);
4. System.out.println(c);  c is local to compute(). Correction: System.out.println(d);

Corrected class:
class Data {
    int result(int x, int y) {
        int z = x * y;
        return z;
    }
    double compute(int a, int b) {
        double c;
        c = a * b * result(a, b) / a;
        return c;
    }
    void display() {
        System.out.println(result(5, 10));
        double d = compute(5, 10);
        System.out.println(d);
    }
}
(Corrected: the source key listed only errors 1 and 4 and missed println(z) and the undeclared d.)

questions.question:
Find the errors in the following program and correct them:
class Simple {
    int a, b, c;
    double sumNumber() {
        int d = a + b + c;
        double e = d / 5.6;
        return e;
    }
    void showNumber() {
        a = 10; b = 20; c = 5;
        System.out.println(d);
        double f = sumNumber(a, b, c);
        System.out.print(e);
    }
}

questions.solution:
Errors:
1. System.out.println(d);  d is local to sumNumber(), so it is not known in showNumber(). Correction: remove it, or print a + b + c.
2. double f = sumNumber(a, b, c);  sumNumber() takes no parameters, so it cannot be called with three arguments. Correction: double f = sumNumber();
3. System.out.print(e);  e is local to sumNumber(). Correction: System.out.print(f);

Corrected method:
void showNumber() {
    a = 10; b = 20; c = 5;
    System.out.println(a + b + c);
    double f = sumNumber();
    System.out.print(f);
}
(Corrected: the source key missed the wrong call sumNumber(a, b, c).)
